  "geometry_notes": "World scale: main chamber is a cylinder of radius 0.55, length 5.0, axis along +X, centered at (-1.0, 0, 0) — left end rounded (hemispherical cap), drawn semi-transparent light gray #E8E8E8 with black outline. Sample inlet nozzle: cylinder r=0.16, length 0.5, on the chamber axis at x=-4.0, gray #B0B0B0. Heater coil: helix r=0.3, 6 turns, spanning x=-3.4 to -2.6 at y=+0.2, copper #B5651D. Sample cloud: ~15 spheres r=0.07, teal #2E9AA0, centered (-3.0, -0.15, 0), spread 0.6x0.3. Electron beam source: dark-gray box #4A4A4A, 0.35x0.9x0.35, hanging below chamber at (-2.6,-1.1,0), emitting a fan of 5 orange #F2A93B rays from (-2.6,-0.6,0) up to the sample cloud. Accelerating lenses: two lavender #9B9BD0 translucent disks, radius 0.5, normals along +X, at x=-1.6 and x=-0.8, each with a small dark triangular aperture wedge at center. Ion beam: 5 teal #2A9AA5 polylines, line width ~2.5px, starting bundled within ±0.06 in y at x=-2.8, straight to x=+0.4. Magnetic field slab: translucent purple #A986B8 at 35% opacity, a slab 2.2 wide crossing the beam diagonally, its plane tilted ~35° from vertical, centered at (1.0, 0, 0). Magnet poles: two gray cylinders r=0.75, length 2.2, axis tilted so it runs upper-left-to-lower-right at ~55° from horizontal in screen space; upper pole centered (1.6, 2.1, 0), lower pole centered (0.4, -2.1, 0), same axis direction, faces #C9C9C9 with darker elliptical end caps. Angled flight tube: cylinder r=0.62, length 3.2, axis pointing right and downward at 22° below horizontal, from (1.0,-0.1,0) to (4.0,-1.3,0), same semi-transparent gray as chamber. Detector: olive-green ellipse #C6CE8B, radius 0.55, capping the tube far end at (4.0,-1.3,0). Ion path curvature inside the field: circular arc of radius R = k*sqrt(m/z)/B with k chosen so that at B=1.0 the Zr-92 path exits along the flight tube axis; masses 90, 91, 92, 94, 96 give five arcs whose exit heights on the detector differ by ~0.16 each, lightest (Zr-90) deflected most (lowest/most-bent path). Bar chart panel: sits to the right of the instrument, plot box 4.5 wide x 3.6 tall; y-axis 0–100 with horizontal gridlines every 12.5 (#CCCCCC), labeled ticks at 0, 50, 100; x-axis categorical positions at 90, 91, 92, 94, 96 spaced proportional to mass. Bar width 0.22, color teal #3FB3B8 (Zr-90 slightly lighter #5BC4C4 as in source), heights: Zr-90 = 51.4, Zr-91 = 11.2, Zr-92 = 17.1, Zr-94 = 17.4, Zr-96 = 2.8. Labels sit just above each bar. Text: black, sans-serif, with thin black leader lines from label to the part it names — 'Sample enters here' and 'Heater vaporizes sample' drop from above onto the inlet and coil; 'Electron beam source' rises from below-left to the dark block; 'Ions accelerated' rises from below to the second lens; 'Magnet' drops from top onto the upper pole; 'Magnetic field deflects lightest ions most' rises from below to the purple slab; 'Detector' rises from below to the olive disk.",
  "notes": "Build chamber and flight tubes as CylinderGeometry with MeshBasicMaterial/MeshStandardMaterial at opacity 0.25, depthWrite false, plus EdgesGeometry outlines in black for the cartoon look; render back faces so the interior beam is visible. Ion paths as Line2/fat lines (THREE.LineGeometry) recomputed each frame from the arc formula so the slider animates smoothly. Magnetic field slab as a thin transparent BoxGeometry with additive-free normal blending. Labels and the bar chart drawn as an HTML/CSS2D overlay so text stays crisp and screen-facing during orbit; the bar chart itself is a 2D SVG/canvas overlay rather than 3D geometry. Panel toggle animates the camera/overlay layout (instrument fills frame, spectrum fills frame, or both at 50% width). Clicking a bar raycasts on the SVG, sets a selected-isotope state that dims the other four ion paths to 20% opacity and shows an annotation callout 'm/z = 90' at the point where that path strikes the detector. Highlight the active bar with a saturated stroke (#00707A) plus a glow on the corresponding detector impact point."
}

